[L1] • I/O—using a socket, connecting to a database, waiting for virtual
[L2] memory swapping, and so on.
[L3] 
[L4] • Processor—numerical calculations, regular expression processing,
[L5] garbage collection, and so on.
[L6] 
[L7] Systems typically have some of each, but for a given operation one tends to
[L8] dominate. If the code is processor bound, more processing hardware can
[L9] improve throughput, making our test pass. But there are only so many CPU
[L10] cycles available, so adding threads to a processor-bound problem will not
[L11] make it go faster.
[L12] 
[L13] On the other hand, if the process is I/O bound, then concurrency can
[L14] increase efficiency. When one part of the system is waiting for I/O, another
[L15] part can use that wait time to process something else, making more
[L16] effective use of the available CPU.
